At Sun, 26 Feb 2006 19:18:03 +0000,
Adrian McMenamin wrote:
> 
> Going through other drivers for ideas to write mine I notice lots of
> non-atomic code where the instructions say there shouldn't be code that
> can sleep eg
> 
> http://sosdg.org/~coywolf/lxr/source/sound/mips/au1x00.c?v=2.6.15#L143
> 
> which includes:
> 
> kmalloc(sizeof(au1000_period_t), GFP_KERNEL);
> 
> In a function called from the trigger callback.
> 
> How important is it that the code is atomic? it would make my life
> easier if it didn't need to be!

The atomicity is important as well as the life isn't always easy.

The trigger callback is designed basically to be executed immediately
(as it name stands).  The jobs that may take long time, i.e. setting
up the hardware to be ready for start, should be done in prepare
callback instead.

In your case, the whole au1000_period_t chunk allocations should be
moved to prepare callback.


